         # Control Flow Guard (CFG) ----------------------------
+        # Not on 32-bit ARM. Two separate things go wrong there. The guard
+        # metadata lld-link produces is wrong, so the loader kills the process
+        # at the first virtual call once it enforces the flag. And asking for
+        # the guard changes how clang emits virtual-call thunks: the thunk
+        # tail-jumps through r1, which is the first argument register, so every
+        # pointer-to-member call on a virtual function arrives with its first
+        # argument overwritten by the callee's own address. r12 is the scratch
+        # register that exists for this. Both are compiler bugs rather than
+        # anything about CFG, and both go away when it is not asked for.
         if (
             c_compiler.type == "clang-cl"
             and c_compiler.version >= "8"
+            and target.cpu != "arm"
